From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Date: Tue, 21 Jun 1994 09:15:23 -0500 (CDT)
Subject: [#94-6-87] SCI: Chaos, Complexity, and Reductionism

James Daugherty:

> > What really is the distinction we want to make with this word? The
> > components of the system interact locally, yet support a global order. ?
> > But all the parts of an automobile interact locally, supporting a global
> > order, yet I wouldn't want to call cars an emergent property of auto parts.
> > Maybe self-organization?
> 
> I think the distiction you are looking for is levels of complexity.  
> Auto parts and the car are on the same level of complexity.  Molecules 
> and atoms are not.  I think self-organization is another issue entirely.

What do you mean by a "level of complexity?"  Why specifically does
the analogy

car : auto parts :: molecule : atoms

fail?  We have discussed emergence in this forum in the past, fairly
interestingly.  I'd love to see another discussion about it.

My take on emergence is that an emergent phenomenon is one in which
a vocabulary change is useful in describing the behavior of an ensemble.
Thus we talk about ocean waves and tornadoes and cells with words
("representation laws" in Newell's terminology) different than those
we use for their components.  Nick would probably think of these
terms as part of a compression process, and there's something to
that, I think.

Two points about this view:

  * It requires an observer interested in "talking about" the phenomena.
    The pure compression view may not require this, but talk about
    the movement of a tornado is only approximate talk, and it's not
    clear to me how we can decide a priori which are the "important"
    parts to be approximated.
  
  * A car *is* "emergent" from auto parts.  It's a particularly dull
    sort of emergence, as it was designed in from the start.  To the
    extent that we identify car phenomena distinct from the carpart
    phenomena, the car phenomena are emergent.  Non-designed emergence
    is more interesting.

derek



