Date: Tue, 21 Jun 1994 15:28:10 -0500
Subject: [#94-6-88] digital cash and free banking

>From: marym@Finesse.COM (Mary Morris)
>At present, I get my paycheck deposited to my bank account electronically.
>I tend to use a debit card for most general transactions. [...]
>My bills are paid through Check-Free, a service that I pay for.
>Very little paper (cash or check) is ever used by me.

Most of the responses (listing various advantages and conveniences of
digicash) to my questions ignored this option. This is the real alternative
to digicash (call it digicheck). The question is anonymous versus
data-trail electronic transfers, not anonymous tranfers versus nothing.
Will we be forced to choose between leaving a data trail at every
transaction, like Mary, or sticking with old-fashioned greenbacks? Are
there enough legitimate users and uses of anonymity for it to get a
foothold or is the black market our best hope for privacy?

-----
>From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
>Only with the suppression of free banking is it possible to sever the
>connection between what we currently call money and "commodity money".

Neither neoclassical nor Austrian theories of money support this
conclusion. How would we test this statement?
[...]
>An understanding of free banking is essential to the conduct of
>institutions which accept deposits of valuable commodities in exchange
>for valuable digital-cash "banknotes".

Digicash issuers do not need an understanding of free banking any more so
than Visa or Check-Free. That is to say, they need none.

>If we back digital cash with FRNs, the game is half-conceded.

How is this different from saying, "if we run a black market with FRNs, the
game is half-conceded?" What is the game, anonymity, fed bashing, or
inflation protection? While I'd be happy to see digicash backed by
something other than the base money of some central bank, I doubt will
happen. While I'd be happy to see central banks go into the history books
like the politburo of the USSR, and happy to see the advent of a more
stable monetary system, I don't see either as inextricably bound to
digicash.

What unit of account will cyberspace adopt? What medium of redemption? The
only reason I hesitate to answer at all is that cyberspace is
international. Businesses that move to cyberspace will continue to list
their prices in their native unit of account and accept their native medium
of redemption after they move to cyberspace and face pressure to use
whatever convention evolves there.  But even so, I'm willing to bet the
answer is U.S. dollars vs. central bank currencies in general, and that
unbacked central bank currencies are more likely than other commodities.

