From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Tue, 21 Jun 94 13:28:58 -0700
Subject: [#94-6-89] digital cash and free banking 

>>Only with the suppression of free banking is it possible to sever the
>>connection between what we currently call money and "commodity money".
>
>Neither neoclassical nor Austrian theories of money support this
>conclusion. How would we test this statement?

Austrian theory as developed by George Selgin supports it. Not all true
statements are subject to empirical testing; this one has merely 
historical evidence. Money was understood to be merely a distinguished
commodity until well after free banking was completely suppressed in 
the MDCs.

>>An understanding of free banking is essential to the conduct of
>>institutions which accept deposits of valuable commodities in exchange
>>for valuable digital-cash "banknotes".
>
>Digicash issuers do not need an understanding of free banking any more so
>than Visa or Check-Free. That is to say, they need none.

They need none only if they are using FRNs, as Visa and Check-Free do.
Visa and Check-Free can rely on the Fed to worry about the stability
of their currency. I stand by my statement, though I phrased it
unclearly; by "valuable commodities" I meant barterable goods 
*other* than FRNs.

>>If we back digital cash with FRNs, the game is half-conceded.
>
>How is this different from saying, "if we run a black market with FRNs, the
>game is half-conceded?" What is the game, anonymity, fed bashing, or
>inflation protection?

It is no different, and the game is all of the above.

>While I'd be happy to see digicash backed by something other than the
>base money of some central bank, I doubt will happen.

I also doubt that the bureaucrats will give us financial privacy just
because we ask nicely. Serial-number tracking technology, which would
make every FRN a marked note, is not far in the future at all. Perhaps
we'll be able to do something with Swiss francs. I concede that for
practical purposes U. S. dollars on deposit are far preferable to gold,
but I don't think this is true of any other currency. Not the yen, not
the mark, not the Swiss franc. In past periods of high dollar inflation,
foreign holders of dollars dumped their dollars for gold in preference
to any competing central-bank currency.

>What unit of account will cyberspace adopt? What medium of redemption?

These questions are unanswerable, because they require predicting the
future. What we need to decide is what units and media *should*
cyberspace adopt? We are not haplessly sitting around waiting for
something to happen to us; *we* are the people who are going to be
designing and implementing these systems. So the question is not 
"what will?" but "what should?".

I think we have every reason to want to dump central-bank-controlled
currencies, and (given the current regulatory state of affairs) few or
no reasons for wanting to preserve them. A commodities-basket would
probably be ideal, but complex and hard to standardize. Gold has the
advantage that technologically-unsophisticated people all over the
world recognize its value (it's an existing standard), plus all the
traditional advantages that led to its becoming the de facto
ultimately-barterable commodity in the first place. I am no gold bug,
but I simply haven't been able to come up with an alternative that I'm
convinced will work better.

Eric Watt Forste     (finger arkuat@netcom.com for the rest of my .signature)

